steps=[step('Derive the gradient bound internally','Finite dimension supplies completeness. Apply the existing quadratic-regularization Hessian theorem with r=0 to obtain LipschitzWith β (gradient V), hence continuity of the actual gradient. This closes a proof ingredient without adding a caller.',r'\|\nabla V(x)-\nabla V(u)\|\le\beta\|x-u\|.','  letI : CompleteSpace','  have hRzero'),step('Identify the zero-safe orthogonal reflection','Total division gives R(0,p)=p. For all n, R is the existing isometric reflection in the orthogonal complement of span(n). Its square is identity and it preserves norm. Expanding the inner product gives its sign reversal; the nonzero branch uses ‖n‖²≠0, while n=0 is handled separately.',r'R_n=(\operatorname{span}\{n\}^{\perp})\text{-reflection},\quad R_n^2=I,\quad\|R_np\|=\|p\|,\quad\langle R_np,n\rangle=-\langle p,n\rangle.','  have hRzero','  have hSmeas'),step('Check Borel and continuous operations','The state-dependent reflection uses continuous gradients, inner products and norms, together with total real division, which is Borel. Thus S is jointly Borel, including h=0. The positive-part rate uses no quotient, so it is jointly continuous and consequently Borel. Joint continuity of S is not required or asserted.',r'S\text{ is Borel},\qquad\lambda=\sqrt\eta\max(0,\langle p,h\rangle)\text{ is continuous}.','  have hSmeas','  have hSlaws'),step('Prove the actual bounce and rate identities','The position stays fixed, so its residual h stays fixed. Reflection involution proves S²=I and norm preservation proves exact H conservation. Its pairing sign reversal gives the flipped rate. For a=⟨p,h⟩, the two sign cases prove max(0,a)−max(0,−a)=a. The zero-residual case gives identity and zero rate.',r'S^2=I,\quad H(Sz)=H(z),\quad\lambda(Sz)=\sqrt\eta\max(0,-a),\quad\lambda(z)-\lambda(Sz)=\sqrt\eta a.','  have hSlaws','  have hHnonneg'),step('Keep zero energy and collect the first nine laws','η>0 makes η⁻¹ nonnegative, so H is nonnegative as the weighted sum of two squared vector norms divided by two. Collect the first nine laws, then fix arbitrary y,xRef,z₀ and z on its actual energy layer.',r'\mathcal E=H(y,x_{\rm ref},z_0)\ge0.','  have hHnonneg','  have hsum'),step('Extract the two energy-layer radii','The equality H(z)=ℰ gives η⁻¹‖x−c‖²+‖p‖²=2ℰ. Dropping each nonnegative summand gives the momentum radius and, after multiplication by positive η, the position radius. Taking square roots requires no ℰ>0 and no division by ℰ.',r'\|p\|^2\le2\mathcal E,\quad\|x-c\|^2\le2\eta\mathcal E,\quad\|p\|\le\sqrt{2\mathcal E},\quad\|x-c\|\le\sqrt{2\eta\mathcal E}.','  have hsum','  have htriangle'),step('Derive the exact pointwise majorant','The triangle inequality bounds ‖x−xRef‖ by ‖x−c‖+‖c−xRef‖. Apply the internally derived β-Lipschitz estimate to h, then Cauchy–Schwarz and max(0,⟨p,h⟩)≤‖p‖‖h‖. Insert both radii and multiply by √η≥0. The resulting Λ is the printed same-energy-layer majorant, not a stochastic path or cost bound.',r'\lambda(z)\le\sqrt\eta\|p\|\|h\|\le\sqrt\eta\,\beta\sqrt{2\mathcal E}\bigl(\sqrt{2\eta\mathcal E}+\|c-x_{\rm ref}\|\bigr).','  have htriangle','\nend\n')]
